6. GPUTypes
---

The DIDE cluster has several GPU types which may be better suited to different workloads, in the table below we summarise the key performance numbers for most machine learning workloads. It is important to realise that these numbers correspond to *peak* achievable performance, in practice many applications will achieve performance substantially below this. Also note that certain accelerated kernels may only be available on newer NVIDIA generations (e.g. Flash Attention 4) and that TF32 cores are default for FP32 workloads. We have the following GPU cards:

* NVIDIA A100 (PCIe):
  * Generation: Ampere
  * Slurm name: nvidia_a100-pcie-40gb
  * Nodes: gpu1 (2 per node)

* NVIDIA RTX A5000:
  * Generation: Ampere
  * Slurm name: nvidia_rtx_a5000
  * Nodes: gpu2 (8 per node)

* NVIDIA L40S:
  * Generation: Ada
  * Slurm name: nvidia_l40s
  * Nodes: gpu[3-7] (4 per node)

* NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000:
  * Generation: Blackwell
  * Slurm name: nvidia_rtx_pro_5000_blackwell
  * Nodes: gpu8 (8 per node)


TFLOPS/s or "tera (trillion) floating point operations per second" is a measure of speed of computation of a GPU. Compute comparisons:

* TF32 Tensor Core (TFLOP/s):
  * NVIDIA A100 (PCIe)     156
  * NVIDIA RTX A5000        55.55
  * NVIDIA L40S            183
  * NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000    130 (estimated)
  
* BF16 Tensor Core (TFLOP/s):
  * NVIDIA A100 (PCIe)     312
  * NVIDIA RTX A5000       111.1
  * NVIDIA L40S            362
  * NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000    260 (estimated)

* FP8/FP4 Tensor Core (TFLOP/s):
  * NVIDIA A100 (PCIe)     Not supported / Not supported
  * NVIDIA RTX A5000       Not supported / Not supported
  * NVIDIA L40S            733           / Not supported
  * NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000    516           / 1032


VRAM is RAM that is built into your GPU and stores anything your GPU is working on. Memory bandwidth is how fast the data can move between VRAM and its processors. Memory comparisons:

* VRAM (GB):
  * NVIDIA A100 (PCIe)     40
  * NVIDIA RTX A5000       24
  * NVIDIA L40S            48
  * NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000    48
  
* Memory Bandwidth (GB/s):
  * NVIDIA A100 (PCIe)     1555
  * NVIDIA RTX A5000        768
  * NVIDIA L40S             864
  * NVIDIA RTX Pro 5000    1344


When you run your GPU jobs, you should consider all these metrics. Some questions you may want to consider:

* How much VRAM does my job need?
* Does my job mainly do computation where TFLOPS/s is more important (compute bound) or does it mainly query large amounts of data where VRAM and memory bandwidth are more important (memory bound)?
* Can I use a lower precision to speed up my jobs or do I need the numerical accuracy?


Some key comparisons: 

* RTX Pro 5000 vs L40S: RTX Pro 5000s have lower TF32 tensor core performance than the L40S however with almost double the memory bandwidth speed they will likely be much faster for memory-bound workloads (e.g. autoregressive rollout of LLMs). Whereas the L40S would do better on compute-bound workloads (e.g. LLM prefill, multiplying large matrices with minimal data movement).

* A100 vs L40S: A100s have similar TF32 and BF16 performance to the L40S with substantially higher memory bandwidth making them a more attractive choice on a *per-card basis*. However, since there are 4 L40S per-node, workloads which employ multi-GPU training with large models/batches will likely do better on the L40S.

* RTX Pro 5000 vs A100: Similar memory bandwidth, and slightly better TF32/BF16 on the A100s. However, the RTX Pro 5000s support FP8 and FP4 natively, making them a much better choice for low-precision LLM inference with VLLM and SGLang.


Recommendations:

Before measuring whether the GPU acceleration is memory or compute bound, try to do determine if there are significant bottlenecks in your code. For example, inefficiences in a dataloader might totally dominate a job's overall performance, making GPU optimisations unhelpful. Once non-GPU bottlenecks have been eliminated or mitigated, try to select your GPU type based on the following things:

* whether its compute or memory bound
* if it can benefit from lower precision (e.g. BF16)
* whether you can increase your batching within a single GPU or over multiple GPUs

A good proxy for your GPU utilisation is to look at the power draw with `nvidia-smi`. GPU power draw however may change at different stages of your job so the following are heuristics for GPU utilisation:

* GPU power draw should not be near idle power draw for extended periods of time. For example, the L40S cards *idle* at around 80W out of 350W, so if your job is running at 90W you are barely utilising the GPUs at all
* Aim to run as close to 100% power draw as possible but above ~50% for most of your job is okay
* If your job has a 100% power draw spike for 10% of its runtime but sits close to idle power draw for 90%, this job is not considered efficient (you probably need to split your job up)
